WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.067
Day 20 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.

00:00:04.617 --> 00:00:08.370
Prompt regression: version, compare and don't break what worked.

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:13.685
Treating every prompt change as a code change: version, side-by-side comparison and

00:00:13.685 --> 00:00:15.981
a regression set that decides the merge.

00:00:17.281 --> 00:00:22.113
A prompt in production is code that gets modified often and almost never tested.

00:00:22.413 --> 00:00:27.845
Someone finds a failing case, adds a sentence to fix it, tries it on that same case,

00:00:27.845 --> 00:00:29.591
sees it works and ships it.

00:00:29.891 --> 00:00:33.330
A week later three cases that used to work no longer do.

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:38.358
Nobody connects one thing with the other, because there's no record of what changed

00:00:38.358 --> 00:00:39.554
or how it was before.

00:00:40.854 --> 00:00:45.872
It's a classic regression problem, and it's solved with the classic tools adapted:

00:00:45.872 --> 00:00:50.830
versions, small changes, side-by-side comparison and a regression set that grows.

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:55.722
This guide builds that flow and adds two cautions specific to models: looking at

00:00:55.722 --> 00:01:00.200
errors by type and watching that prompt examples don't leak from the test set.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.197
To follow this guide you need:

00:01:03.747 --> 00:01:07.105
A golden dataset with test and regression (day 17).

00:01:07.655 --> 00:01:10.096
Two prompt versions in separate files.

00:01:11.396 --> 00:01:12.676
This is the setup.

00:01:12.976 --> 00:01:17.193
Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.

00:01:18.493 --> 00:01:20.759
And this is the next setup file.

00:01:22.059 --> 00:01:24.326
And this is the next setup file.

00:01:25.626 --> 00:01:26.662
Step 1.

00:01:26.962 --> 00:01:30.215
One version, one file, one changelog line.

00:01:30.765 --> 00:01:35.741
Each prompt version is a file, with a changelog line stating which rule changed and

00:01:35.741 --> 00:01:35.981
why.

00:01:36.531 --> 00:01:41.325
Each prompt version is its own file with its number, and each has a changelog line

00:01:41.325 --> 00:01:43.372
stating which rule changed and why.

00:01:43.672 --> 00:01:47.459
Don't overwrite the previous file, because you'll need it to compare.

00:01:47.759 --> 00:01:50.188
The why matters as much as the what.

00:01:50.488 --> 00:01:53.486
"Added rule for performance complaints" isn't enough.

00:01:53.786 --> 00:01:55.739
Add which case motivated it.

00:01:56.039 --> 00:02:00.237
Months from now someone will want to remove that rule, and without the reason they

00:02:00.237 --> 00:02:01.824
won't know what it might break.

00:02:03.124 --> 00:02:04.322
Step 2.

00:02:04.622 --> 00:02:06.227
One change per version.

00:02:06.777 --> 00:02:08.382
One change per version.

00:02:08.682 --> 00:02:12.980
If you change three things at once, you don't know which one moved the result.

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:17.822
If in a single version you rewrite a rule, add an example and change the output

00:02:17.822 --> 00:02:21.788
format, and the result moves, you don't know which of the three moved it.

00:02:22.088 --> 00:02:26.386
One may even have improved things and another worsened them, and you see a tie.

00:02:26.686 --> 00:02:31.298
One change per version means each measurement has a single possible explanation.

00:02:31.598 --> 00:02:32.901
It looks slower.

00:02:33.201 --> 00:02:37.514
In practice it's faster, because you don't waste time untangling mixed changes to

00:02:37.514 --> 00:02:39.484
find the one that caused the problem.

00:02:40.784 --> 00:02:41.867
Step 3.

00:02:42.167 --> 00:02:44.422
Side by side, five times.

00:02:44.972 --> 00:02:50.048
Run both versions side by side on the test set, 5 times, and on the regression set.

00:02:50.598 --> 00:02:55.258
Set up the eval with both versions as prompts in the same file, so they run on the

00:02:55.258 --> 00:02:55.883
same cases.

00:02:56.183 --> 00:03:00.655
Use the test set and the regression set as well, with five repetitions.

00:03:00.955 --> 00:03:05.320
The viewer shows the two columns and lets you filter the cases where one version

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:06.902
passes and the other doesn't.

00:03:07.202 --> 00:03:08.981
Those are the cases to read.

00:03:09.281 --> 00:03:13.436
That's where you see what you fixed, what you broke and what changed its answer

00:03:13.436 --> 00:03:14.961
without changing its verdict.

00:03:16.261 --> 00:03:17.274
Step 4.

00:03:17.574 --> 00:03:19.248
Movement by error type.

00:03:19.798 --> 00:03:23.029
Look at movement per error type, not just the total.

00:03:23.329 --> 00:03:25.677
Fixing one type often raises another.

00:03:26.227 --> 00:03:29.933
The total can stay the same while everything shifts underneath.

00:03:30.233 --> 00:03:34.717
Classify failures by type and compare the count of each type across versions.

00:03:35.017 --> 00:03:38.990
It's common for a new rule to fix one category and worsen another.

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:44.046
An instruction making the model more cautious with complaints, for example, can make

00:03:44.046 --> 00:03:45.632
it under-flag real blockers.

00:03:45.932 --> 00:03:50.266
If you look only at the overall percentage, that trade stays hidden, and you'll

00:03:50.266 --> 00:03:51.693
discover it in production.

00:03:52.993 --> 00:03:54.122
Step 5.

00:03:54.422 --> 00:03:56.433
Leaks between prompt and test.

00:03:56.983 --> 00:04:01.479
Check for leakage: examples you put in the prompt can't be test set cases.

00:04:02.029 --> 00:04:07.276
The examples you put in the prompt can't be test set cases, nor nearly identical

00:04:07.276 --> 00:04:07.604
ones.

00:04:07.904 --> 00:04:12.268
If they are, the model sees the answer before the question and the metric rises with

00:04:12.268 --> 00:04:13.514
nothing having improved.

00:04:13.814 --> 00:04:18.563
It's an easy leak to commit: the failing case is exactly the one you want to use as

00:04:18.563 --> 00:04:19.192
an example.

00:04:19.492 --> 00:04:24.180
Take examples from the dev set or write them from scratch, and verify with a text

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:26.089
search that none appears in test.

00:04:27.389 --> 00:04:28.576
Step 6.

00:04:28.876 --> 00:04:30.156
The merge rule.

00:04:30.706 --> 00:04:35.062
Merge rule: test improves beyond noise and regression doesn't drop.

00:04:35.362 --> 00:04:38.523
Then the newly fixed cases join the regression set.

00:04:39.073 --> 00:04:43.566
Define in advance when a new version replaces the previous one: the test set

00:04:43.566 --> 00:04:48.532
improves beyond noise, meaning no overlapping ranges, and the regression set doesn't

00:04:48.532 --> 00:04:48.828
drop.

00:04:49.128 --> 00:04:51.244
If it qualifies, it merges.

00:04:51.544 --> 00:04:55.052
Afterwards, the newly fixed cases join the regression set.

00:04:55.352 --> 00:04:58.757
That way every solved problem stays protected for good.

00:04:59.057 --> 00:05:03.697
If it doesn't qualify, the version is discarded or worked on further, but it doesn't

00:05:03.697 --> 00:05:05.410
ship "because it looks better".

00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:07.816
An example.

00:05:08.366 --> 00:05:11.875
A ticket classifier marks any slowness complaint as blocking.

00:05:12.175 --> 00:05:16.840
The team creates version seven with a single new rule: performance complaints with

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:18.888
no blocked flow are medium severity.

00:05:19.188 --> 00:05:22.174
They run it against version six, five times.

00:05:22.474 --> 00:05:27.434
On test, the mean rises from 82 to 88 with no overlapping ranges.

00:05:27.734 --> 00:05:32.708
On regression, one case drops: a ticket saying "it takes so long I can't pay" now

00:05:32.708 --> 00:05:33.937
comes out as medium.

00:05:34.237 --> 00:05:35.714
That's a real blocker.

00:05:36.014 --> 00:05:40.206
They adjust the rule's wording in a version eight, run again, and regression is

00:05:40.206 --> 00:05:40.684
complete.

00:05:40.984 --> 00:05:44.725
They merge eight and add four new cases to the regression set.

00:05:45.025 --> 00:05:46.734
The figures are illustrative.

00:05:48.034 --> 00:05:49.697
The most common mistakes.

00:05:50.247 --> 00:05:53.477
Overwriting the prompt without keeping the previous version.

00:05:54.027 --> 00:05:56.584
Putting several changes into a single version.

00:05:57.134 --> 00:06:00.132
Trying the change only on the case that motivated it.

00:06:00.682 --> 00:06:03.784
Using test set cases as prompt examples.

00:06:05.084 --> 00:06:07.328
To know it is done, check this.

00:06:07.878 --> 00:06:11.166
Each prompt version is a file with its changelog line.

00:06:11.716 --> 00:06:15.051
The new version has a single change from the previous one.

00:06:15.601 --> 00:06:19.446
Both versions ran side by side on test and on regression.

00:06:19.996 --> 00:06:22.600
The fixed cases were added to the regression set.

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:25.795
And where AI fell short.

00:06:26.345 --> 00:06:29.192
Adherence to a prompt rule is probabilistic.

00:06:29.492 --> 00:06:32.153
Measure in what percentage of runs it's honored.

00:06:33.453 --> 00:06:34.849
That was day 20.

00:06:35.149 --> 00:06:40.123
The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently

00:06:40.123 --> 00:06:41.131
asked questions.

00:06:41.681 --> 00:06:47.999
Continue with day 21: Testing RAG: retrieval, faithfulness and answer, separately.
